{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"PLAN-REVIEW: BLOCK, 9 blocking findings — and the gate is RIGHT. I wrote the Approach as \"candidate, not\na decision\" and deferred the primary design decision to whoever implements it. That is a placeholder, not\na plan, and it must be resolved before this is claimable. Recording the findings and one genuinely NEW\ndiscovery so the design work is scoped rather than rediscovered.\n\nNEW FINDING FROM THE REVIEW, VERIFIED: a THIRD gate has the same inversion, and my plan never named it.\n`completion.py:332` reads:\n    if cfg.model == DEFAULT_MODEL:\n        cfg = replace(cfg, model=_VERIFIER_DEFAULT_MODEL)\nThat is the same equality test as `review_kernel/verify.py:269`, on a SEPARATE code path (the completion\nverifier's own downgrade). So on any non-default model — every Bedrock id — the completion verifier ALSO\nstops downgrading to sonnet and follows cfg.model. Affected gates are therefore THREE, not two:\nplan-review (via _verifier_cfg), code-review (which has no downgrade at all), and completion.\nNote what sits immediately below that line: a `temperature=0` greedy pin added for bug e458, justified as\n\"an unpinned temperature runs at the provider default (~1.0), whose sampling variance flips borderline\njudgments\". That is the same determinism property ticket 1903 showed is silently lost on opus-4-8 — so a\nnon-default-model run can lose BOTH the sonnet downgrade AND the greedy pin on the same path.\n\nTHE DESIGN DECISIONS THIS PLAN MUST MAKE (currently all deferred):\n1. The replacement semantics for `resolve_verifier_model`. The current rule conflates \"the operator chose\n   a model\" with \"the model is not the framework default\". State the intended rule positively: what\n   distinguishes an explicit operator override from a provider change, given both arrive as cfg.model?\n2. The SCHEMA of the per-provider mapping: key vocabulary (how a provider is named), value shape\n   (pass-name -> model-id? a ladder per pass?), and where it lives (YAML gate file, config table, code).\n3. The DATA-FLOW: how a provider-prefixed id reaches the batch runners. The YAML `model_ladder` is a\n   static list of bare strings flowing unchanged into `production_batch_runner`/`code_review.batch_runner`;\n   the plan must say how per-provider values get there, and how they interact with `resolve_model`'s\n   step > workflow > config precedence and code-review.yaml's per-step `model:` key.\n4. A ROLLBACK/SAFETY story. The Anthropic path MEASURABLY works today (41 opus + 1 sonnet on a real run).\n   Changing verify.py:269 and the model_ladder risks that working behaviour, and the plan offers no\n   revert path or guard.\n5. AC4 is unfalsifiable as written (\"expresses its per-pass models explicitly\") — no artifact, no command,\n   and \"explicitly\" is subjective. Name the observable: which config key, or which grep, proves it.\n6. Positive rationale for \"configuration keyed by provider\" over the alternatives (e.g. runtime\n   provider-aware resolution at the seam, or per-pass model ids carried on the gate YAML). The review\n   correctly notes two structurally different options exist and the plan argues for neither.\n\nSTATUS: NOT claimable. This needs a real design pass, not an implementation pass. It is also the highest-\nleverage ticket in the epic — it blocks BOTH 0d76 (S6) and eb6e (S8) — so the design work is the critical\npath, and doing it badly would be worse than doing it slowly."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785465310370521001,"uuid":"4d117116-b667-4239-998e-6a91fda67918"}